# RED-PROOF + GREEN — search-register-groups.test.ts (S-5, S-6, S-12)

Suite: web/test/invariants/search-register-groups.test.ts
Invariants: S-5 (every search row carries its register label; no register presented as exegesis),
S-6 (staged/quarantined works never surface), S-12 (an empty register group is its own query's
zero, never a truncation artefact) — STUDY_DOCS_DESIGN §8.
Env: APP_DATABASE_URL=app_runtime dev (ep-tiny-hat), DATABASE_URL=neondb_owner dev
(~/.neon_dev_owner_url). Roles verified via current_user before any run.
Fixtures (owner-seeded, prefix `qa-reggrp-`, swept in afterAll): a published HYMN with the group
token once; a published COMMENTARY with the token 40× (outranks the hymn globally — asserted as
the S-12 precondition); a STAGED hymn and a QUARANTINED commentary with their own unique tokens.
All licenses 'Public Domain' so even leaked residue cannot fail Gate B.

The suite tests at the engine layer (lib/search-sections.ts with the catalog fence) — the layer
the W5 merged surface is required to fan out per group (design §2.5/§7.3). The page itself is
W5's file and did not exist when this suite was written; the contract it must honour is pinned
here.

## RED run (a) — S-12 against the anti-pattern (2026-08-13)

The S-12 case first ran written against the FORBIDDEN grouping strategy (review S-C): register
groups as a partition of ONE globally-ranked page at limit=1.

    × S-12 (RED VARIANT — anti-pattern under test)
    → the hymn group must show its own row: expected [] to include 'qa-reggrp-hymn-…'
    Tests  1 failed | 2 passed (3)

The truncated global page hid the hymn exactly as designed — the empty "group" was a truncation
artefact. The case was then rewritten to assert the per-group query (hymn group at limit=1 shows
its row; commentary group likewise), with the global truncation kept as the loud precondition.

## RED run (b) — S-5: the label column removed from the engine

